<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Pure tests mirroring MechaMiner.Content: schema, codec, validation, and
    catalog fixtures. Never launches Godot (docs/technical/91 § Test project
    separation).

    Shared deterministic fixture utilities and the first sample tests are owned
    by TASK-FND-003-001; this file only establishes the project and its pinned
    harness.
  -->

  <!--
    Reflection-based System.Text.Json is off in this test host, matching the
    constraint the shipping hosts run under.

    This is a runtimeconfig.json feature switch, so it binds only where it is
    evaluated for a project that emits an entry-point runtimeconfig.json - a
    HOST. The rule is therefore "every host must evaluate it", not "it must be
    written once per host". Both halves below were measured on this tree, by
    moving the property and diffing the emitted configProperties.

    Setting it on a referenced library does NOT work. A plain library emits no
    runtimeconfig.json at all - MechaMiner.Content builds a .dll and a
    .deps.json and nothing else - and a feature switch does not propagate across
    a ProjectReference. Moving this property onto MechaMiner.Content.csproj drops
    System.Text.Json.JsonSerializer.IsReflectionEnabledByDefault out of all five
    host runtimeconfig.json files; AppContext.TryGetSwitch then reports the
    switch absent and JsonSerializer.Serialize("x") returns "x" instead of
    throwing. That is the move to guard against: it reads as a tidy-up and
    silently re-enables reflection everywhere. (game/MechaMiner.Game.csproj is
    not a counterexample - it sets EnableDynamicLoading, so it does emit a
    runtimeconfig.json.)

    Hoisting it into the root Directory.Build.props DOES work, and an earlier
    version of this comment was wrong to forbid it. Directory.Build.props is
    imported per project, so every host - the four test hosts and
    MechaMiner.Game - evaluates the property and emits it. Measured: the
    configProperties of all five runtimeconfig.json files are identical to the
    per-host arrangement, and reflection-based serialization still throws. A
    shared props file that each host imports is a legitimate place for this.

    Without the switch, a reflection-based JsonSerializer call in a src/ library
    passes under `dotnet test` and throws only in the real host - the exact
    defect class the suite exists to catch.

    Because the binding is invisible in C# source wherever the property lives,
    each test host owes a runtime assertion that the switch is actually live:
    AppContext.TryGetSwitch(
    "System.Text.Json.JsonSerializer.IsReflectionEnabledByDefault", out var
    value) returns true with value == false. It must fail with a message naming
    this MSBuild property and the .csproj or props file carrying it, not just a
    false boolean; whoever trips it moved a project property and has no reason to
    expect a serialization switch to have an opinion about that
    (TASK-FND-003-001). That obligation holds regardless of where the property is
    declared - it is what makes a wrong move loud instead of silent.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <JsonSerializerIsReflectionEnabledByDefault>false</JsonSerializerIsReflectionEnabledByDefault>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../../src/MechaMiner.Content/MechaMiner.Content.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" />
    <PackageReference Include="NUnit" />
    <PackageReference Include="NUnit3TestAdapter" />
    <PackageReference Include="NUnit.Analyzers" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
